# 2. Step-by-Step Setup

## Step A: Initialize Infrastructure
Create the directory tree:

```Bash
mkdir -p ~/brane-central/config/certs ~/brane-central/config/secrets ~/brane-central/target/release
cd ~/brane-central

## Step B: Prepare Files

### B.1 Download Worker Image: 

Download worker-instance-x86_64.tar.gz and place it into ~/brane-central/target/release/.

```Bash
# TODO there is bug branectl try to download from old repo epi-project
branectl download services worker -f
# QUICK FIX: download manually the image 
https://github.com/BraneFramework/brane/releases/download/nightly/worker-instance-x86_64.tar.gz
wget https://github.com/BraneFramework/brane/releases/download/nightly/worker-instance-x86_64.tar.gz -P ~/brane-central/target/release/
```
curl -L https://github.com/BraneFramework/brane/releases/download/nightly/worker-instance-x86_64.tar.gz -o ~/brane-central/target/release/worker-instance-x86_64.tar.gz

### B.2 Generate a backend.yml file

```bash
branectl generate backend -f -p ./config/backend.yml local
```

### B.3 Generate proxy file.yml

```BASH
branectl generate proxy -f -p ./config/proxy.yml
```
### B-4 Generate the Polciy keys

```BASH
branectl generate policy_secret -f -p ./config/policy_deliberation_secret.json
branectl generate policy_secret -f -p ./config/policy_expert_secret.json
```
### B-5 generate  policy database.

```BASH
branectl generate policy_database -f -p ./policies.db
```
### B.6  Generate the node.yml template and move it to the config folder:

```Bash
branectl generate node worker <YOUR_IP> <LOCATION_ID>
mv node.yml ./config/node.yml

### B.2 Secrets: Generate your policy secret:

```Bash
branectl generate secret -f -p ./config/secrets/policy_secret.pem
```

Certificates: Move your ca.pem and client-id.pem into ./config/certs/. Ensure client-id.pem is also placed in a subdirectory matching the LOCATION_ID of the Central hub if you are establishing trust.

## Step C: Certificates

### Client-side certificates

To generate a client certificate, its easiest go to the ./config/certs directory 
and generate the client certificates. Then, you can run:

```BASH
branectl generate certs client <LOCATION_ID> -H <HOSTNAME> -f -p ./client-certs
```

exmaple : branectl generate certs -f -p ./config/certs server client-node-2 -H 145.100.135.241

NOTE: 
 - once the client certificates are generated, copy ca.pem and client-id.pem files 
      with the other clients and the control who intends to connect to this node.
 - similarly copy other clients and control sertificate to your local client
   create a directory named certs/client-node-x/

### Step D:  Writing policies

- make sure you have a Policy Database: Initialize the local policy store (see Step B.5)

- Generate Policy Token: Transfer the policy_token.json (signed by your Central node) to the home directory:

```Bash
branectl generate policy_token <INITIATOR> <SYSTEM> <DURATION> -s <PATH_TO_SECRET>
```

example: 
branectl generate policy -f -p ./config/policy_deliberation_secret.json
branectl generate policy -f -p ./config/policy_expert_secret.json

cp ~/path/to/received/policy_token.json ~/brane-central/policy_token.json
```

NOTE: copy  the policy_token.json (signed by your Central node) to the home directory:

``BASH
cp ~/path/to/received/policy_token.json ~/brane-central/policy_token.json
```
## Step D: Launch

### Validate the configuration, then start the service:

```Bash
# Validate the setup
branectl -n ./config/node.yml check

# Start the worker
branectl start worker
```
# 3. Verification
 - Verify the system is operational by checking the active containers and logs:

- List Containers: docker ps (You should see brane-worker and brane-chk running).

- Check Logs: docker logs brane-worker (Verify there are no "Connection Refused" or "Auth" errors).

